ype/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e048c1c1052238e83f1c74864e4806371e0fa8c2c2f3499b6e244614f14aeb76.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392672} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1f5f2-9232-5218-9446-6da440aee018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392673} {:answer/id #uuid "c565c814-24a3-5363-a559-bd3be2f5b15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/546afec9a47772181b933cb26afda052e5f039226c02db0c565b3e06b0418458.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392674} {:answer/id #uuid "f4966d18-8ba2-5a56-8bd4-5d1a3b25f287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/025a9032c02e58fba378b41936f3f7191251bd14ba1e63c2d199a5352ce7494b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392675} {:answer/id #uuid "f919a43e-ea4c-5056-bf0d-7a796ccc14a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d11a3557cddc21f5f20f4c85374fa2ca7bddf70307671542f228874af64585d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1f5f2-9232-5218-9446-6da440aee018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392673} :answer-field/id #uuid "df901850-5b4c-5250-a9cd-0821b7975975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f7cda04-70ae-570b-9bd1-8ebb0e1d2784" :question/math-academy-id "q-49165" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](images/2f/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75f1ac34-ae96-53af-b8ae-d465af28f75d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Nonlinear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03586d0c-f5e8-5e69-b892-c66dec4bac13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329992} {:answer/id #uuid "4d981e05-f204-5746-b563-1200bf7029c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi -10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330656} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac1756e-6f46-5db6-b81a-0f93dabf39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331339} {:answer/id #uuid "b32cd351-5bbb-5b14-886d-1a782a3ec03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331532} {:answer/id #uuid "db780350-77de-59e7-9cb8-44a2789581e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db780350-77de-59e7-9cb8-44a2789581e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331894} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e88743f-f334-5f02-80cc-27798abfc36c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee52113a-602c-5626-9b5c-a32cb2cd5215" :question/math-academy-id "q-49205" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6f61767434332bbb92574ff87d72bda4b07028d77bda5925f73b32a7c751d5.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(2,-2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+lw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2\\pi -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2-2\\pi -8 \\\\  & =-2\\pi -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e499c456-6a1c-5152-933c-28134d048ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396682} {:answer/id #uuid "f947c798-bc89-571a-a666-d5ac5fe1b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396683} {:answer/id #uuid "085d6ab0-e2f9-55ab-a353-c2e9ceb0a9dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396684} {:answer/id #uuid "6da93e3c-bfd8-566a-bf85-bf589185d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396685} {:answer/id #uuid "b72fbfe6-34a0-5341-a7d6-2590a001b91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e499c456-6a1c-5152-933c-28134d048ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396682} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2a4470-ddb9-5a4f-aab9-d2401a7ffff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a62fd18-f127-592a-8fe3-1cc5a47bb7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49234" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3541e9d4-b110-5c63-a736-7fc7f4d31791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330422} {:answer/id #uuid "86df8515-e1e9-50e5-8ffa-c0f477c898b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331153} {:answer/id #uuid "9dec32d8-d9c0-5660-8378-5288983a6ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331364} {:answer/id #uuid "a3922fe0-a979-577b-8dba-fd55ff68e732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331416} {:answer/id #uuid "f1373522-1826-5a96-a8aa-2b1480b28e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86df8515-e1e9-50e5-8ffa-c0f477c898b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331153} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc984231-74ce-5ee3-a0d9-26f2e71c9fc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c589cab7-d081-524b-ae47-ab787d74dcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49273" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16897289-4603-55b3-85a9-7a4e51b12fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330138} {:answer/id #uuid "351bd75d-098f-5b2a-b526-a98d2a025590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330419} {:answer/id #uuid "50b59dc3-9cc4-5d56-8dae-d5fde6823b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{tan}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330681} {:answer/id #uuid "9532f3f6-9b3e-5893-931d-79b72c8db1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{csc}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331272} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8968e8-f099-5f73-8828-0a36b59f6c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8968e8-f099-5f73-8828-0a36b59f6c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7347c5-9c24-53ba-ae9d-867009e5aca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f71f24a-4751-5945-a98f-4a541dbf6232" :question/math-academy-id "q-49275" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b95828-a685-54a8-96b4-b52ae657e912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330153} {:answer/id #uuid "444fa90c-41cd-594e-acf1-cfca43d9f868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330563} {:answer/id #uuid "4e61c48b-d79c-5d63-9602-758c8c834289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330664} {:answer/id #uuid "7f11ee4d-4f0b-51c9-8e3d-549f4a158d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331072} {:answer/id #uuid "89b1ee96-afb9-5e02-a824-2992fac6b5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444fa90c-41cd-594e-acf1-cfca43d9f868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330563} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f414d51-cc19-5057-95ed-395de2e5b540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3ad6824-baa3-5500-876c-87beb97cf8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-49277" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{-1}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c90047e-5401-5ba8-a53b-d862cffbe449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330204} {:answer/id #uuid "466188e9-4d22-5129-87b9-e7a328c808e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330585} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebfcb94-8843-58bf-83d6-6669a9d3f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331067} {:answer/id #uuid "85bab4ce-d6e2-5a7e-b957-3e4d1dfb39fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331137} {:answer/id #uuid "e4af34c3-195c-5e90-88c0-7159576b42c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ebfcb94-8843-58bf-83d6-6669a9d3f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331067} :answer-field/id #uuid "6411dc61-38a7-5873-95f1-abc2652e8c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72054613-827c-572f-aba3-072d29c357bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49309" :question/problem "![](images/af/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9279f99b-07e2-5a92-8769-3475c1ea0dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} {:answer/id #uuid "b87fe0d4-fd32-5c26-8c08-409e0cde8ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331580} {:answer/id #uuid "c30a6019-ea0e-584b-a717-eb7354915f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331684} {:answer/id #uuid "d274239f-73bc-5926-8b40-d5d950a40492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331817} {:answer/id #uuid "fad18f17-4495-5b82-8fa3-74b112c945db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9279f99b-07e2-5a92-8769-3475c1ea0dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce4ab34-42a1-5b78-9fe0-f9fc91c4a733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f052148-475f-5e9d-b934-2204e76a0ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-49315" :question/problem "A bicycle tire has a pressure of $6\\,\\text{psi}.$ It is being inflated, and the pressure is increasing at a rate of $4t\\,\\text{psi}$ per second, where $t$ is a time, in seconds, since the start of the inflation. The tire has a slow puncture that reduces pressure at a rate of $(t+2)\\,\\text{psi}$ per second. Identify an expression for $P(t),$ the total pressure in the tire at any time $t\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)=4t-(t+2)=3t-2$ be the rate at which the pressure in the tire is changing. Note that we subtract $(t+2)$ to indicate the rate at which the pressure is decreasing.\n\nLet $P(T)$ be the pressure in the tire after $T$ seconds. Then, using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t)|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0ba6d15-a20e-5f16-b324-0bbead5458ca" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Cosecant-Cotangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008298da-bf7a-51ca-837d-ab329d0489ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329955} {:answer/id #uuid "099d6ddf-d5f2-5fd1-9989-6721d1ea7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330034} {:answer/id #uuid "88ac8b32-2829-5bde-aed3-69f279dfedfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331164} {:answer/id #uuid "97b2c286-d293-598e-8173-52cb4aaa72e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331301} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c3c459-f04c-5677-8215-e3b74b9824f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2c3c459-f04c-5677-8215-e3b74b9824f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331681} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2910a34-30ac-5541-b445-de1db206308c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d70cb193-c0f3-511e-a986-3ca7b8cef3f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49332" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{1-{cot}^{4}⁡x}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{1-{cot}^{4}⁡x}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ad216da-1bcf-5b87-9436-918ef1e5015a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Bounded Above a Curve and Below the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6cf138-6dd7-556c-bff2-d4bf324c0349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330201} {:answer/id #uuid "422fa2db-f8da-5f5c-a0d1-16fc8bf3aab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330540} {:answer/id #uuid "8c550e50-e081-54d1-b207-66d1e0d435f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331195} {:answer/id #uuid "98b9fd8d-9175-53a9-a6ba-858749487bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331313} {:answer/id #uuid "9e45e7f1-44ff-5977-b6ca-b1007824b25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c550e50-e081-54d1-b207-66d1e0d435f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331195} :answer-field/id #uuid "b239e446-7043-541d-830c-5b0c62532760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4ab101e-16fa-5b23-a91e-323666dba44a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49348" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd73ecb5633b2d3e43637ab5be7dacb6cd69d96957fbc199b0d59bbe6dbbcbec.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis between $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, as shown above. What is the area of the region?" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1-(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39877dcd-c15f-5b42-9992-628fd69aa8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330466} {:answer/id #uuid "4d37ea75-59a6-53b7-8119-27c921f17e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330649} {:answer/id #uuid "78fde374-d249-56e5-8ed2-413b17b19765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331025} {:answer/id #uuid "bde7299b-9c7d-563b-a909-63879e3087e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331631} {:answer/id #uuid "fae341a7-c701-5ce9-8756-3f3183adee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde7299b-9c7d-563b-a909-63879e3087e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331631} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8664ab-82c6-5935-885b-4704ea3140b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec1f8b08-73c8-5230-ba02-f32931218a96" :question/math-academy-id "q-49354" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & -10 \\\\  & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+7}{x+2}=5-\\frac{3}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5-\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228ee8e3-4cbc-54ca-86e0-41d6984f52f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(1+x)(1-x)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330248} {:answer/id #uuid "415b24a1-a0a1-566e-804a-10c3a545d297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(1+x)(1-x)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330529} {:answer/id #uuid "4af1094c-11cd-5b21-a560-c386722e9007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330628} {:answer/id #uuid "5e33cd57-a4d3-57fa-af23-86332f408cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330791} {:answer/id #uuid "86a4a011-da14-5843-825b-84a1ae516e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86a4a011-da14-5843-825b-84a1ae516e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331147} :answer-field/id #uuid "d573eb9f-5b4a-5c4d-be89-c86d1a19944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1dc5a85a-4afe-5495-af86-22c90f6982a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49360" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{1+x}+\\frac{1}{1-x})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+x}+\\frac{1}{1-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d518d37-c7ae-5a1f-a0d7-975836876bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int ({u}^{6/5}-{u}^{1/5})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330068} {:answer/id #uuid "22416b44-6976-5c1a-98e0-b980f491c8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {u}^{6/5}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330243} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9f6d49-cb40-5e76-b341-e228fcbb45f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {u}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330346} {:answer/id #uuid "c9966b80-b9d5-57ac-825e-ea0bad9d0755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int ({u}^{6/5}+{u}^{1/5})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331733} {:answer/id #uuid "e11b0cfa-c722-5383-90f9-ba1ea47846a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int ({u}^{1/5}+{u}^{-1/5})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9966b80-b9d5-57ac-825e-ea0bad9d0755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int ({u}^{6/5}+{u}^{1/5})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331733} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed8005f-5fb3-571e-8c62-3dc3d9cb1264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d725487a-5806-5f7e-95bc-a9839bf05cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49386" :question/problem "Given that $u=x-1$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int x\\sqrt[5]{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u=x-1,$ then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=u+1.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt[5]{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (u+1)(\\sqrt[5]{u})(\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int (u+1)({u}^{1/5})(\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{6/5}+{u}^{1/5})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027ccbbd-79ba-5d67-8599-c6645b801c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}+{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329987} {:answer/id #uuid "844f0a0b-95d9-53ea-83cb-1576d7223aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331125} {:answer/id #uuid "91b35456-97f5-5ca0-a329-1699c5d10e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331248} {:answer/id #uuid "db77fa6b-b561-58ac-b36c-844e170a1f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331893} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac5c0c-f8e1-538e-a058-d5226be5735b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844f0a0b-95d9-53ea-83cb-1576d7223aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331125} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc4d9a6a-230e-54a2-a466-e038c2c827ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0542bde-b2c9-57c4-9ab2-47066e6c42b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49387" :question/problem "Given that $u=1+{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u=1+{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n${x}^{2}=u-1$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (u-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739b7d37-465f-53a0-92fd-a46f218e891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398871} {:answer/id #uuid "c966cc5b-2f5d-56fa-94a2-562519ed3dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398872} {:answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} {:answer/id #uuid "bede226b-b6b5-5601-a51f-99f2392a59d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398874} {:answer/id #uuid "c91eab72-8ce7-50be-b8c3-3258720d8056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570962e4-656c-562b-b969-b8505698bd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398873} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f41607-89e1-5a62-888e-441db9b42ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d0396e-82eb-542b-a43f-b6df87f217ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49431" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(1-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{1-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(1-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(1-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(1-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70657675-2db0-5a10-9cf9-052f05edf016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} {:answer/id #uuid "3909aff4-3810-5b83-850f-f988be7ba4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398879} {:answer/id #uuid "4061afb3-02dc-5f4e-ae19-437ea14959c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398880} {:answer/id #uuid "3295113f-e7ef-59d3-92a7-66e977a1b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398881} {:answer/id #uuid "732c775a-c5f4-5d4e-91c3-0fdfab8cfb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70657675-2db0-5a10-9cf9-052f05edf016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398878} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad56171-faf0-523d-b089-53ed18731d86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61d8dd43-99c6-5162-9111-9fc96bb88dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49439" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=x-100.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x-100)^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{7/4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{11/4}}{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}(x-100)^{11/4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{4}{11}\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}+C] & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{11}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(x-100)^{11/4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{4}(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-100)+0 \\\\  & =(x-100)^{7/4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[4]{(x-100)^{7}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19a857ac-d36b-5753-a532-4f2a75612d85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108a7ce4-2fbf-516f-a9ee-b0a3967d1ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi +25{\\pi }^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330097} {:answer/id #uuid "47bebd14-566b-5a48-8dbc-247b8097cc88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330597} {:answer/id #uuid "60ddca83-6f18-5dc0-95bb-2dc02a3ce78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330809} {:answer/id #uuid "c27ccaf5-3c10-5e24-8068-3a5fefe8b53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -5{\\pi }^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331679} {:answer/id #uuid "e52dff9e-56c4-5804-958b-c767f612d3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ddca83-6f18-5dc0-95bb-2dc02a3ce78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330809} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bb69ecc-629f-586f-a88d-4e843428cff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7b11093-5b5a-58f1-bca1-3927cf2df9a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49447" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10\\cdot 1-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(10x-{x}^{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}]-[10\\cdot 0-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8b60ab-4072-5f31-94dd-75c493f8372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330332} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6534ec-42dc-57fa-b80f-40c681fa5d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331061} {:answer/id #uuid "ad30acb9-e2fc-5534-870a-032790c3f83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331484} {:answer/id #uuid "e1762878-a949-5466-848b-82eaa3555015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331941} {:answer/id #uuid "f51b2f83-41fe-5b84-b9cb-eee7ef105dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1762878-a949-5466-848b-82eaa3555015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331941} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3093a8-9026-569a-a294-190db04689b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "503a6da2-cd33-5244-b696-2a607d8e13a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49451" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064206 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc4294d6-3d60-5c9e-a862-863e1d129049" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a085972-1baf-5648-867f-fa22e0ba0ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330175} {:answer/id #uuid "333a8449-f54e-51ed-9268-4a29c687d042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330401} {:answer/id #uuid "903c6656-2cfe-57a6-9d04-bf4558a5f1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331233} {:answer/id #uuid "b84ce15d-f916-5272-a13b-c0e2002f591a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331578} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f98b80-b189-5486-9209-b6f6b685306a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "333a8449-f54e-51ed-9268-4a29c687d042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330401} :answer-field/id #uuid "e906895e-8048-51d8-952c-ac1ed3e3bf3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7ef9efb-7fe5-57f8-b97b-c7d8ad3002b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49453" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ad2ded-1acd-5d0f-9482-ff2d40cddfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-3x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330459} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ce5c3-0e37-5bf1-b40e-9f1752a34f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330901} {:answer/id #uuid "7c615908-c6e2-5115-bb4e-9bf4a5d0c8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331043} {:answer/id #uuid "9fda2557-86c6-5fb7-959b-9fbd45ed4623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331387} {:answer/id #uuid "f5494940-6b67-5796-a605-3c01ce2a03d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ce5c3-0e37-5bf1-b40e-9f1752a34f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330901} :answer-field/id #uuid "78373e7d-b109-554d-afe9-f989789a365a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67660e4b-92f2-5ae6-aaa9-23b06f12a0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-49456" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f1bb19-f6a2-538a-8094-300b1e55cc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398885} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8f42a5-cc05-5072-93b3-0b55ad819b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398886} {:answer/id #uuid "1c531862-1246-5d33-83db-faf2c5f53bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398887} {:answer/id #uuid "eeef3e88-28df-595f-88f1-2280a46131c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398888} {:answer/id #uuid "c04d34f3-85b9-5e8c-b8f6-dc61a84a62ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04d34f3-85b9-5e8c-b8f6-dc61a84a62ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398889} :answer-field/id #uuid "69629315-77cc-510c-b6be-75d926e5d966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2058ea27-d95b-5f37-8b22-5c724bfe6f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49468" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=s+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2/5}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(s+1)}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[{(s+1)}^{2/5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s+1)+0 \\\\  & ={(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94ad8efa-33ee-5451-b6b9-c99cb81a9ba2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32797f-9d7a-5b52-8960-235626e6cc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}({u}^{1/2}+{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330067} {:answer/id #uuid "16e90fc0-413f-571e-9234-9c9d29c6bffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330146} {:answer/id #uuid "7c647bff-6963-5d64-a559-cc69b3e75a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331044} {:answer/id #uuid "ed05fcfb-bb70-599b-838c-5723fd5d7864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332028} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a4b5a-7c6a-584d-ab83-e74c1159cbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed05fcfb-bb70-599b-838c-5723fd5d7864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332028} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96e4fa0-68d1-58eb-a210-73f341888124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2063239-3a6f-54f6-9552-f4b8aed0756c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49529" :question/problem "Given that $u=2x-1$, which integral is equivalent to ${\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u=2x-1$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{u+1}{2}.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $5$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\sqrt{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{u+1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(u+1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94ad8efa-33ee-5451-b6b9-c99cb81a9ba2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4025429-6e17-5c14-a483-024d71ec6616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392691} {:answer/id #uuid "906bbb28-e4b5-5434-be5a-c275368e56e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392692} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb1e0bc-fcd0-5a9a-a1c2-f002323abc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392693} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5df1c7-ea7d-5d47-a8d0-092ad3a409bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392694} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d7aa84-cc44-5f47-a62a-eef3f56d5530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5df1c7-ea7d-5d47-a8d0-092ad3a409bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392694} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a3d3edd-4727-5c6c-b8de-2aa598d8b805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1acd046b-858c-598a-a588-48bb2b8b4ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-49532" :question/problem "Given that $u=3-2{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to ${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u=3-2{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{4}=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that ${x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(3-u).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3-u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d0c2f0-e1ef-5f9d-b277-1d69053a1f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330265} {:answer/id #uuid "5d04e998-1dc4-5619-8be2-aa1e5c1b7c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330779} {:answer/id #uuid "866ac174-f329-56ce-b375-426c38e8089c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331143} {:answer/id #uuid "a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331438} {:answer/id #uuid "d944c1b0-1445-5a57-b346-1c4580f6e918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331438} :answer-field/id #uuid "7066a7e5-a4e7-571e-8102-812757ff24f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf21744c-e558-571b-b3ed-9b3f13019f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-49538" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3f469d-d785-54c0-8500-e4f4c541c311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330478} {:answer/id #uuid "44fe7e54-fa6e-59d5-8a26-f4f36bb5501f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330572} {:answer/id #uuid "848d8e79-5f9b-50c9-a7a2-c023a98ccebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331128} {:answer/id #uuid "be85cd80-7bd6-5b67-8980-2e785a3889ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331634} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4584df-c775-5f4a-a59c-6d2f35a59525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be85cd80-7bd6-5b67-8980-2e785a3889ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331634} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5cefbe-1794-5312-8f1f-f28eb36bbc55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ff7c3e1-6732-5fb3-bcd2-7ed1aeab0712" :question/math-academy-id "q-49540" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo. let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edb1c73-87ee-58c2-a730-43e7f693d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330078} {:answer/id #uuid "26074d64-e780-5986-be16-fa82ceeb0164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330281} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec2d31d-3894-565f-9b91-3bc180c6d541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330506} {:answer/id #uuid "581c2751-ecf3-5021-a41f-a828e408e3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330735} {:answer/id #uuid "59dbc6dd-87a6-5e93-99fe-8c88f23299aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec2d31d-3894-565f-9b91-3bc180c6d541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330506} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef0aa3c5-1f7e-5feb-b2ae-0f439aa2181d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea76ea1f-1600-5c9d-b198-b9fb197580ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-49548" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0218ebee-f46b-52a3-af27-002bd2e16fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329977} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0a098d-fa78-5f98-bfe7-2e76cd1fe7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330349} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1c856a-d31e-5281-a2a6-f20cbee86a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330499} {:answer/id #uuid "6da04d85-0964-58d1-bd80-e9e62694fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330930} {:answer/id #uuid "e57ddc07-4a02-59ea-b4b1-dd526c6b3d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da04d85-0964-58d1-bd80-e9e62694fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330930} :answer-field/id #uuid "d448c3a1-6bd6-56fc-a1f7-e15ec5601f57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e79ee700-e1a2-5377-9595-991fdfcefa49" :question/math-academy-id "q-49551" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{64\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/64)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/64}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (8\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0edf81-9914-5a0f-b9b6-d58d70540093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330630} {:answer/id #uuid "70cf07b2-a48c-5c20-b1ad-493648041c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330962} {:answer/id #uuid "9a56a3d4-f0db-5dee-8e1b-e1e7174dd979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331338} {:answer/id #uuid "a43c7a93-7056-56e0-adc4-8cc368c5eac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(9x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331423} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c453d1-3b22-5ee0-8261-9dc453f931aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8c453d1-3b22-5ee0-8261-9dc453f931aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331996} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ccce72-f1f2-5395-8899-d57e02ead31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "751e8889-464b-57e5-9446-01158bf809c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49554" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $3x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fdd384-a572-5724-9364-8acb408f2ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329976} {:answer/id #uuid "40ccecfa-a656-5109-b074-472b0a8ab1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330526} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4aa74e-0e4c-5d1a-93ae-9302fd3645d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330757} {:answer/id #uuid "b994301b-7078-571a-b444-f5890625ea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331590} {:answer/id #uuid "e6504bdb-2c3c-5430-a7f9-f5f85facc1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fdd384-a572-5724-9364-8acb408f2ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329976} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c2b40b-2f58-56ca-bd6c-3ed7effb446f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82a7ff27-052c-59e7-b715-3cb92743a1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49570" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{n}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{x}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{x}{3}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37a7aff6-d1b2-5383-bb26-aa6363594b05" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f44a31c-8f02-517f-a3ff-76a016ef1103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330368} {:answer/id #uuid "3b888702-3835-5e64-a746-f342b2add188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330480} {:answer/id #uuid "55e0e2dc-0727-59f1-ab88-5104534b5eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330719} {:answer/id #uuid "570539a7-4903-5aaa-8ab5-396b0db3ecbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330727} {:answer/id #uuid "fe377b72-3be3-5386-8d19-dd14073a8aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e0e2dc-0727-59f1-ab88-5104534b5eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330719} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c0ce28-8066-581a-a24f-804e0ab448c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131ef8ba-67f1-5c9d-a9d6-716f27012aa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49605" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$  gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {u}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258ec5da-ec83-55fd-a7fe-8f38d4ead2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330272} {:answer/id #uuid "28087732-5391-5b65-ae41-76ed0de34484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330307} {:answer/id #uuid "82583c9b-6934-531e-bb06-247d73a3e2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331101} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8ba72c-b7fe-5fd5-bdca-da017f4acd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331350} {:answer/id #uuid "cff74fb2-4786-5e4f-9c0a-839e99bc59dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c8ba72c-b7fe-5fd5-bdca-da017f4acd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331350} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f49af48-cb5e-5a12-81ec-da6258d149ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "353a67b7-0856-52c7-a290-d3c8b2b07314" :question/math-academy-id "q-49609" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dcde3b5-5cdf-5266-9392-0835053a7ded" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157cb123-7330-58bc-a063-3374ccd593b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330122} {:answer/id #uuid "15ed61b4-3ade-50d3-8b2c-9f81770e4827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{95}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330135} {:answer/id #uuid "25977eb2-b160-5400-965b-3c3bab2cb7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330274} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcb3619-33fc-5423-ae01-62b75b557302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331039} {:answer/id #uuid "e0809338-7fdb-55c8-b1a8-c0195030c113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0809338-7fdb-55c8-b1a8-c0195030c113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331931} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f9ab705-9785-5102-b86c-ad670d149651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17c3d123-f91a-5a92-a651-8b2492b2be43" :question/math-academy-id "q-49610" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{(2x+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2x+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(2x+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{3}-{3}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{125-27}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e50061-c0cd-514f-8589-ebd50e9d1e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330101} {:answer/id #uuid "15d95872-0f46-51e2-b153-2442cc2ccf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330132} {:answer/id #uuid "99340278-5d40-5f93-b26f-a88c525e06b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331320} {:answer/id #uuid "b6603c76-d9c0-5622-8266-b3996ec50863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331559} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a90fb1-443f-5acc-8cf7-2a64602260da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99340278-5d40-5f93-b26f-a88c525e06b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331320} :answer-field/id #uuid "2509a479-fd42-562e-8cab-f44c6d32a5f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67f65475-64ee-5851-9243-07c4ff50e013" :question/math-academy-id "q-4962" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(-2)=1$ and ${g}^{′}(1)=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
